# Git Branch & PR Workflow

Prefer stacking many changes on a **single feature branch** and pushing them
all to **one PR**. Don't create new branches on your own, and never push
directly to `main`.

For PR contents and management (issue links, title/labels, test plan, adding
work to an existing PR), see `git/pull-request.mdc`.

## Don't create new branches unless asked

Stack work on the current feature branch instead of spinning up new ones.

### Always do

- Keep committing to the **current branch** for new work, even when it's a
  different feature, fix, or refactor than the last one.
- Push to the current branch so the existing PR updates (see
  `git/pull-request.mdc`).

### Never do

- Run `git checkout -b ...` / `git switch -c ...` / `git branch <name>` unless
  explicitly asked for a new branch.
- Split work onto a separate branch "to keep it clean" without being told to.

### Exceptions

- The user explicitly asks for a new branch (e.g. "make a branch for this",
  "put this on its own branch").
- You're currently on `main`/`master` with no feature branch. Work can't stay
  there, so **create one yourself** off an up-to-date `main` (see below)
  rather than asking which branch to use — name it for the work, following
  the repo's existing branch-naming convention, and **say which branch you
  created**. Don't commit the work on `main`.

### Base a new branch on an up-to-date `main`

When a new branch **is** warranted (either exception above), create it from a
**fresh `main`** — not stacked on top of another feature branch.
Stacking branches tangles history and produces messy, duplicated PR diffs.

#### Always do

- Update `main` first, then branch from it:

```bash
git switch main
git pull            # fast-forward main to origin/main
git switch -c <name>
```

- Base the new branch on `main` even when your current checkout is a
  different feature branch.

#### Never do

- Run `git switch -c <name>` / `git checkout -b <name>` while on another
  feature branch, so the new branch inherits that branch's unmerged commits.
- Branch from a stale local `main` without pulling first.

### Cleaning up the old branch when starting a new one

When the developer explicitly asks for a **new** working branch, consider
deleting the current one — but only when it's safe, and never silently:

- Delete the current branch **only if** it is **up to date with `main`**
  (all its commits are already in `main` — e.g. `git branch --merged main`
  lists it, or `git log main..HEAD` is empty). Its work is already merged,
  so the branch is disposable.
- If it has **any** unmerged work (not up to date with `main`), do **not**
  delete it — keep it.
- **Always ask the developer first** and get explicit confirmation before
  deleting, even when the branch is up to date with `main`. Never delete a
  branch unprompted.

## After the developer merges the PR

When the **developer tells you the PR was merged**, clean up locally and sync
`main` so the next branch starts fresh. The developer's "it's merged" statement
**is** the explicit go-ahead for deleting the now-merged local branch —
satisfying the confirmation requirement in "Cleaning up the old branch when
starting a new one" above, with no separate ask needed in this flow.

### Always do

- Sync `main` and delete the merged feature branch:

```bash
git switch main            # if not already on it
git pull                   # fast-forward main to origin/main
git branch -d <branch>     # safe delete: refuses if not fully merged
```

- Remain on the updated `main`. New working branches then branch off this fresh
  `main` — see "Base a new branch on an up-to-date `main`" above.

### Never do

- Delete a branch with **unmerged** commits. Use `git branch -d` (not `-D`) so
  the tool itself guards against dropping unmerged work.
- **Force** the deletion with `-D` when `git branch -d` refuses. That refusal
  means the branch isn't actually merged — **stop and report** instead of
  forcing it.

## Never push directly to `main`/`master`

All changes reach `main` (or `master`) through a pull request that a human
reviews and merges.

### Always do

- Create a feature branch for your work (e.g. `git checkout -b fix/device-list-500`).
- Push the feature branch: `git push -u origin HEAD`.
- Open a PR for review (e.g. `gh pr create`). For PR contents and management
  (issue links, title/labels, test plan, adding work), see
  `git/pull-request.mdc`.

### Never do

- `git push origin main` / `git push origin HEAD:main`
- `git push --force` to `main` (or any force push to `main`).
- Committing on a local `main` branch and pushing it upstream.

### If you are already on `main`

Move your work to a branch before pushing:

```bash
git branch fix/my-change      # capture current commits on a new branch
git checkout fix/my-change
git push -u origin HEAD
gh pr create
```

If the user explicitly asks you to push to `main`, stop and confirm; warn that
it bypasses review, and only proceed if they clearly insist.
